    <p class="lede first reveal">"Blogging is dead" is the most repeated wrong thing in this industry. It gets said by people who tried it for three weeks, posted a diary entry no one searched for, and concluded the medium was the problem. It wasn't. They were writing the wrong thing and calling the genre deceased.</p>
    <p class="reveal">Here is what's actually true. Blogging didn't die — it changed jobs. It stopped being a place to post your day and became the most durable traffic asset a small business can build: an article that answers a real question keeps getting found, for years, by exactly the people looking for it. Quietly, it's still the closest thing to an unfair advantage a beginner has.</p>
    <p class="reveal"><strong>You do not have a "blogging is dead" problem. You have a "what to write" problem.</strong> Write for a search someone is already making, and a single article can outwork a year of social posts. The medium was never the issue. The topic was.</p>

    <div class="plate-photo reveal">
