    <div class="chapter reveal"><span class="bignum">01</span><div class="num">Chapter One</div><div class="orn"><span class="d"></span></div><h2 id="dead">Why blogging isn't dead — it changed jobs</h2></div>
    <p class="answer reveal">Blogging isn't dead; it stopped being a personal diary and became a search asset. A blog post that answers a question people actively search keeps bringing readers for years, long after social posts have scrolled away.</p>
    <p class="reveal">The difference is where the traffic comes from and how long it lasts. A social post peaks in a day and vanishes. <strong>A well-targeted article compounds — it ranks, gets found, and keeps working while you sleep, for months or years.</strong> That's not a dying medium; that's the most patient, durable asset in the whole business.</p>
    <div class="box tip reveal"><span class="tag"><span class="g">✦</span> The Edit</span><p>The clearest way to think about it: social media is renting attention by the day; blogging is buying an asset that pays rent forever. One resets to zero every morning. The other accumulates. For a business built to last, the choice makes itself.</p></div>

    <div class="triptych offset reveal">
      <div class="photo mask img" style="background-image:url(/images/library/detail-hands-typing-cinematic.jpg);background-size:cover;background-position:center"></div>
